# WeaveFFI Wasm (experimental)

This folder contains a minimal stub to help you load a `wasm32-unknown-unknown` build of your WeaveFFI library.

Build (example):

```bash
cargo build --target wasm32-unknown-unknown --release
```

Then serve the `.wasm` and use `weaveffi_wasm.js` to load it.

## Complex Type Handling

Wasm only supports numeric types natively (`i32`, `i64`, `f32`, `f64`). Complex types are encoded at the boundary as follows:

### Records

Records are **plain JavaScript objects**. A record crosses the boundary serialized in the WeaveFFI value-buffer format as one pointer + length pair in linear memory; the glue packs and unpacks it automatically, so there are no handles, no accessor functions, and nothing to free.

### Enums

C-style enums are passed as **`i32` values** corresponding to the variant's integer discriminant. Rich (algebraic) enums are plain objects tagged by variant name (`{ tag: "Circle", radius: 2 }`) and cross the boundary serialized in a value buffer, exactly like records.

### Optionals

Optional values map to **`null`** for the absent case. An optional crosses the boundary inside a value buffer as a one-byte presence flag followed by the value when present. The one exception is an optional interface, which stays a nullable object pointer (`0` = absent).

### Lists and Maps

Lists are JS arrays; maps are plain objects (a `Map` instance is also accepted on input). Both cross the boundary serialized in a value buffer as one pointer + length pair, recursing through element types.

### Iterators

`iter<T>` functions return a **lazy JS iterator** (typed `IterableIterator<T>`): each `next()` issues exactly one producer call, so iteration streams in constant memory. The producer handle is destroyed exactly once, on exhaustion or via `return()` when iteration stops early (a `for...of` loop calls `return()` automatically on `break` or `throw`). Abandoning an iterator without exhausting or closing it leaks the handle.

### Callbacks and Listeners

Each listener surfaces as a `register.../unregister...` pair. `register` takes a plain JS function and returns a numeric subscription id; `unregister` takes that id and stops delivery. Delivery is **synchronous and same-thread**: `wasm32-unknown-unknown` is single-threaded, so events fire only while a call into the module is on the stack (for example, a producer function that emits during its own execution). A producer that emits from a spawned thread cannot run on this target at all.

Callback arguments are **borrowed for the duration of the callback**: strings, byte buffers, and buffered values (records, rich enums, optionals, lists, maps) are copied or decoded into JS values before your function runs, and interface arguments wrap producer-owned objects. Read what you need inside the callback and do not retain an interface wrapper or call `free()` on it.

### Error Handling

The generated JS wrappers automatically handle errors by passing an error
pointer as the last argument to each Wasm function. The error struct is
16 bytes on wasm32: `{ i32 code, char* message, uint8_t* payload_ptr,
size_t payload_len }`. Your Wasm module must export the following
functions:

- `weaveffi_abi_version() -> i32`: the ABI revision the module implements; the loader refuses a mismatch
- `weaveffi_alloc(size: i32) -> i32`: allocate `size` bytes in linear memory
- `weaveffi_dealloc(ptr: i32, size: i32)`: release a `weaveffi_alloc` block
- `weaveffi_error_clear(err_ptr: i32)`: clear and free error resources
- `weaveffi_free_string(ptr: i32)`: free a producer-returned C string
- `weaveffi_free_bytes(ptr: i32, len: i32)`: free a producer-returned buffer

Wrappers of functions declared `throws` raise the declaring module's typed
error class (a `WeaveFFIError` subclass with a per-code subclass, such as
`KeyNotFound`); every other wrapper raises the generic `WeaveFFIError` only
for producer panics and marshalling failures. When the matched error code
declares payload fields, the wrapper decodes them from the error's value
buffer and attaches them as properties on the thrown error.

## API Reference

### Module: `docs`

#### Error Domain: `DocsErrorsError`

Throwing wrappers in this module raise `DocsErrorsError` (a `WeaveFFIError` subclass); each code below is its own subclass carrying the stable `code`. Payload fields are decoded from the error's value buffer and attached as properties on the thrown error.

| Class | Code | Default Message | Payload Fields |
|-------|------|-----------------|----------------|
| `NotFound` | `1` | Document not found | (none) |
| `InvalidStatus` | `2` | Invalid status transition | (none) |

#### Functions

##### `weaveffi_docs_create_document`

Create a brand new document.

The document starts in `Draft` status; callers must publish
it explicitly to make it visible.


`weaveffi_docs_create_document(title: i32, tags: i32, i32) -> i32, i32`

| Param | API Type | Wasm | Notes |
|-------|----------|------|-------|
| `title` | `string` | `i32` | NUL-terminated C string pointer |
| `tags` | `[string]` | `i32, i32` | value buffer: ptr + len in linear memory |
| _returns_ | `Document` | `i32, i32` | value buffer: ptr + len in linear memory |

##### `weaveffi_docs_get_document`

Look up a document by identifier

`weaveffi_docs_get_document(id: i64) -> i32, i32`

| Param | API Type | Wasm | Notes |
|-------|----------|------|-------|
| `id` | `i64` | `i64` | native Wasm i64 |
| _returns_ | `Document?` | `i32, i32` | value buffer: ptr + len in linear memory |

##### `weaveffi_docs_publish_async`

Publish a document in the background

`weaveffi_docs_publish_async(id: i64) -> i32`

| Param | API Type | Wasm | Notes |
|-------|----------|------|-------|
| `id` | `i64` | `i64` | native Wasm i64 |
| _returns_ | `bool` | `i32` | 0 = false, 1 = true |

#### Interfaces

##### `Notebook`

An ordered collection of documents.

Notebooks own their documents and release them when destroyed.


Passed as an **opaque object pointer** (`i32`), wrapped by a JS class. Constructors return an owned handle; methods pass the handle as the implicit leading `self` argument; `free()` releases the handle via the destroy symbol.

##### `weaveffi_docs_Notebook_new`

Create an empty notebook

`weaveffi_docs_Notebook_new() -> i32`

| Param | API Type | Wasm | Notes |
|-------|----------|------|-------|
| _returns_ | `Notebook` | `i32` | opaque object pointer |

##### `weaveffi_docs_Notebook_insert`

Add a document, returning its position in the notebook

`weaveffi_docs_Notebook_insert(document: i32, i32) -> i32`

| Param | API Type | Wasm | Notes |
|-------|----------|------|-------|
| `document` | `Document` | `i32, i32` | value buffer: ptr + len in linear memory |
| _returns_ | `i32` | `i32` | native Wasm i32 |

##### `weaveffi_docs_Notebook_find`

Look up a document by identifier, failing when none matches

`weaveffi_docs_Notebook_find(id: i64) -> i32, i32`

| Param | API Type | Wasm | Notes |
|-------|----------|------|-------|
| `id` | `i64` | `i64` | native Wasm i64 |
| _returns_ | `Document` | `i32, i32` | value buffer: ptr + len in linear memory |

##### `weaveffi_docs_Notebook_size`

The number of documents currently held

`weaveffi_docs_Notebook_size() -> i32`

| Param | API Type | Wasm | Notes |
|-------|----------|------|-------|
| _returns_ | `i32` | `i32` | native Wasm i32 |

##### `weaveffi_docs_Notebook_max_documents`

The largest number of documents one notebook will hold

`weaveffi_docs_Notebook_max_documents() -> i32`

| Param | API Type | Wasm | Notes |
|-------|----------|------|-------|
| _returns_ | `i32` | `i32` | native Wasm i32 |

##### `weaveffi_docs_Notebook_destroy`

Releases the object reference. Called by the wrapper's `free()`.

#### Structs

##### `Document`

Represents a single document tracked by the system.

Documents are persisted to disk and exposed via the public API.


A plain JS object, serialized in a value buffer (`i32` ptr + `i32` len) at the boundary. Fields in declaration (wire) order:

| Field | Type |
|-------|------|
| `id` | `i64` |
| `title` | `string` |
| `status` | `Status` |
| `tags` | `[string]` |

#### Enums

##### `Status`

Lifecycle status of a document.

Used to drive UI badges and filters.


Passed as `i32` discriminant.

| Variant | Value |
|---------|-------|
| `Draft` | `0` |
| `Published` | `1` |
| `Archived` | `2` |

